## 3. Mixed paths — how the boundary is applied

Some files legitimately serve both products. The rule is: **the license follows
the module's purpose, and an import never relicenses the imported module.**

- **`src/cli.ts` / `src/cli-admin.ts`** — the CLI was split so the Apache-2.0
  entrypoint contains no proprietary command and no import of
  `src/server/**` or `src/store/**`, not even a dynamic one. `src/cli.ts` calls
  `import('./cli-admin.js')`; the proprietary extension registers `serve`,
  `tenant`, `key`, `plan`, `audit` and `bind-installation`. When the extension
  is absent (Apache-2.0-only distribution) the entrypoint registers stubs with
  the same command names that explain what is missing and exit non-zero. The
  scan commands keep working unchanged.
- **`src/scanner` dependencies consumed by the server** — `scan.ts`, `notify/**`,
  `github/**`, `report/**` stay Apache-2.0; the hosted server is allowed to
  consume them under Apache-2.0. The reverse is not true: Apache-2.0 code must
  not depend on proprietary modules to run.
- **`data/**`** — this is the offline snapshot the CLI needs to scan without
  network access, therefore Apache-2.0. The hosted feed service that publishes
  fresh records is proprietary and lives outside this repository.
- **`tests/**`** — a test follows the license of the module it exercises. Tests
  that import `src/server` or `src/store` are proprietary (see §2).
- **Licensing is not a technical access control.** Nothing in `src/server` or
  `src/store` becomes open source merely because it is distributed in the
  repository or referenced by Apache-2.0 code.

